--TEST--
`new C(...)` is `E0740` and `$m->method(...)` on a `mixed` receiver is `E0732`, never a panic — ADR 0027 § 1 gives the `(...)` spelling to a member, so the two shapes that name no member are refused where they are written
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Box {
    public int $n;
    public function constructor(int $n) { $this->n = $n; }
    public function get(): int { return $this->n; }
}
// ADR 0027 § 1's kept list is a list of *members*, and a constructor is
// not one: `new` names a class, and the closure this syntax builds
// carries a callee rather than an allocation. PHP refuses the same
// expression, so refusing it is the compatible answer as well as the
// only one with a meaning.
Box $b = new Box(...);
// The opposite failure, and why the two take separate codes: this one
// names a member and has no class to bind it to. ADR 0036 § 4 defers a
// *call* through a `mixed` to the receiver's runtime class, but a
// closure value outlives the site and carries its callee with it.
mixed $m = new Box(1);
callable $f = $m->get(...);
// The control: both spellings ADR 0027 § 1 keeps resolve a member, so
// neither refusal fires on these two and the expectation carries no
// diagnostic for either line.
Box $c = new Box(2);
callable $g = $c->get(...);
callable $h = Core\Str::length(...);
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0740]: `new` has no first-class callable form
   --> %s:12:10
   |
12 | Box $b = new Box(...);
   |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^ `Box` is constructed here, not called
   = help: ADR 0027 § 1 gives the `(...)` spelling to a member — `Class::method(...)`, `$obj->method(...)`, `self::method(...)` — and a constructor is not one. Write the closure out: `fn (): T => new T(…)`

error[E0732]: `get(...)` names no closure through a `mixed` receiver
   --> %s:18:15
   |
18 | callable $f = $m->get(...);
   |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^ a closure value is named here
   = help: ADR 0036 § 4 defers a *call* through a `mixed` to the receiver's runtime class, but a closure value carries its callee with it and there is no class here to read one off — call the member directly (`$m->get(…)`), or narrow the receiver first with `instanceof` or `as ClassName`

error: aborting due to 2 errors
